+    production:
+    - CliPresence.alreadyProvided
+    - CliArtifact.inHome
+    - HomeCloner.sanctionedParentShim
+    tla: []
+    adapters: []
+    test_graph:
+    - home-integrity/EveryShimResolves
+    workflow_data: []
+  found_by: 'Wave 2, attempting to provision HIS-4''s declared worktree; recorded as DEF-004 and escalated.
+    THE FIRST DIAGNOSIS WAS WRONG and was corrected by the owner''s sanity check -- see corrected_root_cause.
+    The original framing ("verify and clone disagree about a sanctioned shim") described a real symptom
+    but named the wrong cause and would have led to changing the sanction rule, which is not the defect.
+
+    '
+  superseded_diagnosis: 'Filed first as: home verify sanctions five bin/cli symlinks into the root home
+    while home clone refuses the same five as FOREIGN_HOME, so no ticket worktree can be built. Both halves
+    of that are TRUE and neither is the cause. The asymmetry is correct behaviour: the project home IS
+    a registered child of root, so its parent-store shims are sanctioned; a fresh clone is not yet anybody''s
+    child, so they are not. Root-pointing shims are BY DESIGN -- sanctionedParentShim exists to bless
+    them and the artifact graph models them as cli-shim:* artifacts with producers.
+
+    '
+  corrected_root_cause: "THE REBUILD IS A NO-OP AT THE SHIM, AND REPORTS SUCCESS.\nMeasured on a clone\
+    \ of this project home, with the raw epic build:\n\n  skill-manager build --force cli-shim:skill-script/tlc2\
+    \ cli-shim:skill-script/computeq\n  ✓ cli: installed computeq -> <clone>/.skill-manager/bin/cli/computeq\n\
+    \  ✓ built cli-shim:skill-script/computeq\n  2 selected: 2 built, 0 already current, 0 not buildable\
+    \ here\n\nand afterwards:\n\n  <clone>/bin/cli/computeq -> /Users/hayde/.skill-manager/bin/cli/computeq\n\
+    \nunchanged, still pointing into root, and `home verify` on the clone still refuses all five. The\
+    \ command announced a write it did not perform -- the same defect shape as #133 finding 3, one layer\
+    \ down.\nWHY. CliPresence.alreadyProvided (CliPresence.java:137-150) short-circuits on providedByThisHome,\
+    \ whose question is CliArtifact.inHome -- \"will this run from this home\". A symlink at <home>/bin/cli/<name>\
+    \ pointing into ANOTHER home runs. So it is \"provided by this home\", the installer skips the write,\
+    \ `build` reports built, and the one property that makes the shim wrong -- that it belongs to a different\
+    \ home -- is invisible to the check deciding whether to replace it.\nSo this is the epic's theme once\
+    \ more, in a new place: TWO ANSWERS TO ONE QUESTION. `inHome` answers \"runnable from here\"; `verify`\
+    \ and `clone` answer \"owned by here\"; nothing makes them agree, and a home can therefore be simultaneously\
+    \ complete (nothing to build) and unusable (five foreign paths).\n"
+  consequence: 'The state cannot self-heal. A cloned home carries shims it does not own, `build` refuses
+    to replace them because they run, and `home clone` refuses the home because they are foreign. bootstrap-home.sh
+    therefore fails and neither `wt new` nor `skt ticket new` can produce a ticket home in this repository
+    -- which is how the defect was found.
+
+    '
+  slice: 'TWO HALVES, and the owner settled the second one -- see the_contract_the_owner_stated.
+
+    (1) THE DEFECT. Make presence ask about OWNERSHIP as well as runnability. A path in this home''s bin/cli
+    that resolves into another Skill Manager home is not provided by THIS home, whatever it runs. Today
+    it is, so a change-driven rebuild silently does nothing and says it did something. This half is required
+    under either design and is the actual bug.
+
+    (2) THE CLONE. Do NOT build eagerly, and do NOT convert the inherited shims into cold refusals. The
+    clone keeps them usable -- they are the parent''s artifacts, they run, and the agent needs them on
+    PATH -- and DECLARES them: what artifacts this home has, which unit each belongs to, and the exact
+    command to rebuild one after changing that unit. Then `home clone` must stop refusing a home for holding
+    exactly what it was designed to inherit.
+
+    An earlier draft of this slice offered "either the clone rebuilds them locally, or it declares them
+    cold". Both are wrong: the first spends time rebuilding artifacts nobody changed, and the second takes
+    working tools off the agent''s PATH to prove a point about ownership.
+
+    '
+  the_contract_the_owner_stated: 'LAZY BY DESIGN, AND THE AGENT IS TOLD. "The whole point of introducing
+    artifacts into skt was to make the agent aware of the artifacts, the CLI, the things to be built.
+    So we clone, then we say: here are the artifacts for these skills on the path for you, and here is
+    how you rebuild them if you make a change. Why would we build them without the agent changing? That''s
+    just a waste of time. However, if the agent changes it, then they''ll rebuild."
+
+    So the clone is a DECLARATION, not a build, and the rebuild is CHANGE-DRIVEN. That is why (1) is the
+    whole defect: the change-driven rebuild is the only thing that ever replaces a shim, and it does not
+    work.
+
+    '
+  acceptance:
+    assertions:
+    - '`build --force` on a shim that points into another home replaces it, or says plainly that it cannot
+      and why.'
+    - No command reports an artifact built when the path it names is unchanged.
+    - bootstrap-home.sh produces a usable ticket home from this project home, and HIS-4's worktree can
+      be created.
+    - A CLI genuinely provided by the system, outside every home, is still not reinstalled — the existing
+      escape hatch survives.
+  goals:
+  - goal: GOAL-home-invariants
+    contribution: direct
+    expected_effect: 'one new invariant stating that an artifact reported built is an artifact whose output
+      this home owns, with a regression cfg that fails when a foreign path satisfies presence
+
+      '
+    local_signal: python skills/test_graph/scripts/run.py home-integrity
+  - goal: GOAL-no-spurious-holdback
+    contribution: guard
+    expected_effect: must not weaken the rule that stops a home reaching into another home's bytes
+    local_signal: jbang RunTests.java
